f us is staying home because  you get you know to get to get full-time  child care for that number of little  kids it's like a teacher oh yes daycare  yeah yeah it's it's no joke and so i did  stay home  and um but  i wrote  those books while i was staying home  with those kids at that time with a  bunch of little kids and people would  ask  how do you  do it like i mean how why would you add  more stress to your plate to have these  book deadlines and and i got a pretty  big deal with harper collins for my  second third books and that involved  deadlines and pressure  but what i always tell people is when  you find that thing  that you are called to do a friend of  mine calls it your blue flame when you  find that blue flame work that you're  meant to do  not only does it not feel like work but  it gives you energy i had more energy to  be a mom to i had three kids in diapers  for mo the better part of a decade three  kids in diapers at once because i have a  baby as soon as you know the older one  got out of diapers and so it you know  kids don't sleep through the night i  mean it was  it was very difficult physically  but what got me through it was doing  that writing having to do a chapter  having to have that that chapter  finished by friday it made me  get up a little earlier in the morning  it made me keep the kids on a schedule  so i could have that nap time to do my  writing and i i really believe that it i  was a better mom for the fact that i had  those writing deadlines during that time  okay okay that's interesting so i think  one of the things i struggled with when  i had scarlett  was  my mom was a stay-at-home mom um so it  was just my brother and i but she stayed  at home until i think we were in grade  seven and then she became a massage  therapist went to massage school like  full-time  but i had  i think what happened to me was i was in  university i had scarlet and then i  stayed  home  for a period of time like when she was  really really little and  i didn't  i felt guilty doing anything that didn't  have that seemed like it didn't have  anything to do with her so at that point  it was just like any type of writing or  like blog stuff i think i was just  writing a blog at that point or working  for my dad i was like  and i think it was partly  some people around me who are like  oh you should be only staying at home  and then there were the other people who  were like  you're wasting your life because you're  only staying at home but how do you like  do you have  suggestions or  anything for i guess mothers  who are trying to figure out how much  time they should be spending on  themselves when they have little kids  yeah and that's what you know you  mentioned that sketch i did about  traditional mothers and that was exactly  the point that i was making in that  sketch um so  to give you a little bit of background  to lead into my answer to what you just  asked so i had the benefit of uh i guess  it was about 15 years ago at this point  i interviewed some relatives of mine who  were born in around like 1910 1912 and  they were in their late 90s at the time  and i just asked them about life i  didn't even know what i was getting at  but we just we did some video we set up  the old heavy  camcorder and videoed this and at the  time i had just had a baby and so i  didn't  intend for these to be interviews about  motherhood but i was so stressed out  because my kid didn't sleep through the  night and and i just was really maxed  out about it so that's just where my  mind went and i ended up asking my  grandfather and then his his cousin and  some of these other people i talked to i  parenthood just kept coming up and one  of the things that was very clear to me  and keep in mind these people you know  they were in their 20s in the 1920s and  30s so they knew people as adults who  lived in the 1800s i mean they were  really connected to an old old way of  life  and one of the things they emphasized is  how busy mothers were i mean when you  think about it i've been joking about  this on my podcast  if your kids wanted chicken nuggets  any time before like the past 50 years  it was up to you to take it from chicken  to nugget that was all on you and think  about how time-consuming that is you're  slaughtering animals you're doing all  that stuff to dress them you're chopping  it up i mean this to just get dinner on  the table was an hours long process and  so when you look at the history of  humanity when you look at human  evolution it simply could not be the  case that human children have evolved to  need their mother's attention for all or  even 90 or even 80 of her waking hours  because the work of survival was very  very time consuming for our female  ancestors and that's the principle that  i've always used in my home of course  your kids need to be your priority and  they they need your attention and they  need focused attention but this is not  all day every day it couldn't be because  that's what i said in that in that  sketch that you saw traditional mothers  were busy and so if you are busy doing  your you're writing a book that's  similar to your ancestors who were busy  going slaughtering animals like getting  them ready for cooking yeah the work  you're doing is different but the time  commitment is about the same  okay that's interesting i guess some  people i think i think this was an  incorrect way of the way i was thinking  about it but it was because it didn't  have anything to do you know it was work  but it wasn't work for the kids so if  you're like trying to survive and  chopping wood and slaughtering chickens  then it's like okay that's directly for  the family but  i i know i certainly felt better when i  had time put aside for work because i'm  like i'm very you know work oriented  fulfills me you find a project to work  on and it's great right uh so  it was tricky though i definitely had a  hard time  being like no even though i'm doing  something that isn't related to that  it's okay yeah i was like deeply  ingrained in me as the wrong thing to do  yep and i think we all struggle with  that that there yeah of course there is  a question of okay i don't have to write  this book the way my great-grandmother  had to  wash the clothes because everyone only  had three pairs of clothes but to me it  it seems like  i don't think it matters as much to your  children  what  work you're doing i i think it just  feels natural that yeah mom has  stuff going on and for our ancestors  that might have been washing clothes out  on the washboard for us it might be  writing but i i don't think that it  that it matters to the children as long  as they know that  you  that they are your priority
 

